          <p>This case study about Shopware development and operating costs shows how an individually designed online shop also pays off for startups and small businesses, and how they can profit from my experience as an e-commerce developer.</p>

          <p>First, a few words about myself: As a web developer, I have co-developed and successfully managed numerous e-commerce projects, including large online shops, over the long term, including Shop-Apotheke and clients from the OTTO group. Since 2024, I am a certified Shopware developer and extension partner. In this case study, I show how I apply my experience to small shops and the development of custom themes as well, and how that pays off for my customers.</p>

<h3>Case Study about E-commerce Development and Operating Costs at a Glance:</h3>
<ul dir="ltr">
  <li><strong>Role:</strong> Full-stack and certified Shopware developer</li>
  <li><strong>Effort:</strong> ca. 3 weeks / 120 hours</li>
  <li><strong>Time</strong>: 2024 to 2026 (as a research project parallel to other projects)</li>
  <li><strong>Tech stack:</strong> Shopware 6.7, PHP 8.2, SCSS, Twig</li>
  <li><strong>Client</strong>: me (open source reference project)</li>
  <li><strong>Goal: determine the total costs of an individual shop installation.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>Result: a two-figure, one-off four-figure budget achievable in principle.</strong></li>
</ul>
<h2>Challenges</h2>
          <p>Founders, startups, and other small shop operators often struggle to run an online shop that is customized
            yet also accessible and legally compliant. In turn, major platforms' offerings can rarely be adapted
            sufficiently to one's own requirements, or they can become a long-term cost trap.</p>

          <p>Between unsatisfying AI and construction set solutions on one hand, and high-price projects for major clients on the other, there are practical solutions at reasonable prices. However, it is difficult to present reliable figures on total costs based on a specific customer project, as such data are usually subject to confidentiality for good reason. Demo projects rarely get finished in favor of actual client projects in the day-to-day operations of an agency or a freelance developer.</p>

<h2>Implementation: from Research Project to Merchandise Shop</h2>
          <p>I started the implementation as a professional development project to prepare for my certification as a Shopware developer. I didn't finish the custom theme until a good two years later, and I've been running it in production since 2026. This includes full responsibility for legal and software security, payments and deliverability, as well as regular maintenance. In a similar way, a good fifteen years earlier, my blog website had initially started out as a test installation for WordPress. From that angle, WooCommerce as an e-commerce software would have been the obvious choice. However, Shopware seemed superior to me for several reasons, especially concerning stability, loading time, and customizability. Furthermore, even the free and open-source Community Edition is already prepared for the German market.</p>

          <p>First, I developed a custom theme on a test server, picking up the blog design using the same fonts and colors. Additionally, I integrated the <a href="https://store.shopware.com/en/ingos57544164693f/cost-transparency.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shopware plugin for Cost Transparency</a>, which I had already released as a free extension in the official Shopware store. Thus, the costs for individual plugin development are not included in this case study's total cost calculation. The presented shop serves as a live demo for the plugin I developed and as a test environment under conditions that are at least comparable to the operations of a small startup.</p>

          The results obtained so far can be viewed in specific detail at <a href="https://shop.open-mind-culture.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">shop.open-mind-culture.org</a>.

          <p>All products are actually available for delivery. Unfortunately, the supply chain transparency ends from my perspective when I purchase goods from other manufacturers as a reseller. However, the price transparency chart clearly shows my slim margin and what proportion of the costs is estimated for logistics, such as shipping to my clients.</p>

          Honestly, and that also belongs to being transparent with such a project, there are unfinished issues, errors, and improvement suggestions. Those are, like the theme's source code, openly visible in the <a href="https://github.com/openmindculture/sw-IngoSOpenMindCultureTheme" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub repository</a>. The same open source principle applies to my cost transparency plugin. Both are work in progress. For example, the Lighthouse Audit only shows 77 of 100 "best practices" points, and the accessibility check shows insufficient color contrast in one area.

